				<key>WFCommentActionText</key>
				<string>Lock Signal Probe — standalone diagnostic.

Wire this shortcut into two Personal Automations for ONE test app: "App Is Opened" passing the text OPEN, and "App Is Closed" passing the text CLOSE. Then exercise the phone by hand: switch apps, lock the screen with the app in front, unlock, go to Home Screen, and so on.

Every run appends one JSON line to the file PROSOCHE/lock-signal-probe-log.jsonl inside the Shortcuts folder in iCloud Drive, recording the time, which signal arrived, and whether the device was locked at that instant. Open the Files app and read that file to review the results. Comparing the CLOSE lines that carry locked true against the ones that carry locked false tells you whether iOS fires the App Is Closed trigger on screen lock the same way it fires on an app switch.

This build deliberately uses a plain text file instead of a Note. Notes actions popped a picker on device and wrote nothing. This shortcut shows no alerts, dialogs, pickers or results, so it never interrupts you while running in the background.</string>
			</dict>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionIdentifier</key>
			<string>is.workflow.actions.comment</string>
			<key>WFWorkflowActionParameters</key>
			<dict>
				<key>WFCommentActionText</key>
				<string>Shortcuts generated by Shortcuts Playground. May contain mistakes. Always check the shortcut's actions first.

This shortcut was created via the following user prompt:

&gt; Rebuild the "Lock Signal Probe" diagnostic shortcut for the PROSOCHE project from scratch, replacing its unreliable Notes-based logging with file-based JSON-lines logging. Abandon Notes entirely for this probe. Log to a fixed-path JSON-lines file instead, using this project's own already-proven, device-verified file I/O pattern. It accepts OPEN or CLOSE as Shortcut Input from a Personal Automation (or nothing when run by hand), reads Get Device Details for Device Is Locked, formats the current date to the second, builds one single-line JSON log entry, appends it to the running log file and saves it back. No UI anywhere: no Show Result, no Show Alert, nothing that requires the user to dismiss anything.</string>
			</dict>
		</dict>
